course: dictionary
lesson: 6754
title: Words from a cup of tea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things you may never have tasted. Context means the words around a word, and they often give clues. «All real tea, green, black, white and oolong, comes from one plant» puts oolong in a list with other teas, so you know it is a kind of tea.
explain: Look for more clues. Oolong sits between green tea and black tea in taste and colour. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your guess in the sentence, and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Oxidised is a hard word, but the dictionary explains it: the leaves are left to react with air. A cut apple going brown works the same way. Partly oxidised means only part of that change has happened, which is why oolong lies in the middle.
fact: Oolong means a kind of Chinese tea that is partly oxidised.
fact: Oxidised means left to react with air, which changes the flavour and colour.
fact: Oolong is grown mostly in China (Fujian) and Taiwan, and is often served in small cups.
fact: «All real tea comes from one plant, Camellia sinensis.» The difference is how the leaves are dried, rolled and left to darken in air.
exercise: «All real tea, green, black, white and oolong, comes from one plant.» What is oolong? | A fruit from a tall tree | A type of soup | *A kind of Chinese tea | A city in the mountains
exercise: Oolong lies between green tea and black tea. What does between suggest about its taste and colour? | *It is in the middle of green and black | It is sweeter than sugar | It has no colour at all | It is the same as milk
exercise: The leaves are left to react with air. What does oxidised suggest? | They were cut into tiny pieces | They were frozen in ice | They were washed in sugar water | *They have darkened after reacting with air
test: Where is oolong mostly grown? | Mexico and Peru | Egypt and Norway | *China and Taiwan | Italy and France
test: What do all teas, including oolong, come from? | A grain called rice | Many different plants | *One plant, Camellia sinensis | Seaweed
exam: What makes oolong different from green and black tea? | The size of the teapot | Whether it is served cold | *How the leaves are dried, rolled and left to darken in air | The colour of the cup
